{"id":"33173b08-b067-48dd-871b-8bcb0de7d3a3","object":"chat.completion","created":1785026352,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    # map id to node\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    # build children map\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(p, []).append(n['id'])\\n    # sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    ids_sorted = sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for nid in ids_sorted:\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        # never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # check retained children\\n        has_retained_children = any(cid not in removed for cid in children.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_children:\\n            continue\\n        # tentative removal\\n        removed.add(nid)\\n        # check required symbols still represented\\n        # compute set of symbols still present\\n        present_symbols = set()\\n        for n in nodes:\\n            if n['id'] not in removed:\\n                present_symbols.add(n['symbol'])\\n        if required.issubset(present_symbols):\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            removed.discard(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed])\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removal_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\\n\"\n}\n```","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that performs a grammar-guided node reduction. The problem: given a tree (nodes with parent, symbol, depth, removable flag, utility), we need to reduce it by tentatively removing nodes (depth-first, deeper first) while preserving required symbols. The output should have removed_node_ids (list of IDs removed in order) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of IDs of nodes that remain).\n\nKey constraints:\n- Never remove root, required symbol nodes, or nodes with retained children.\n- Only remove if removable flag is True.\n- After tentative removal, check that all required symbols remain represented (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains).\n- Use node ID as tie-breaker for equal depth (stable tie). Should sort by depth descending, then ID (string) ascending? The description says \"deeper nodes before shallower\" and \"use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth\". So we process nodes in order: by depth descending, then by ID lexicographically ascending? Or descending? Usually \"tie break\" means when depths are equal, use ID to decide order. The phrase \"stable tie break\" suggests deterministic order. Need to infer from typical ordering for tree reduction: we want to try removing deeper nodes first, and among same depth, maybe by ID ascending to be deterministic. But we need to check the removal order in output: \"the actual removal order\". So we need to simulate the reduction process: iterate over nodes in some order, tentatively remove if conditions met, and if removal successful, add to removed list, and remove from tree for subsequent checks. After processing all nodes, we have final retained nodes. Then sort retained IDs (presumably ascending? The output schema says array of strings, but doesn't specify order. However the description says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So retained_node_ids should be sorted (likely ascending). The removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed.\n\nWe have to implement the algorithm. We'll parse case['nodes'] into a dict mapping id to node dict. Build children mapping. Process nodes in order: depth descending, then ID ascending? Let's examine the fixture: The expected behavior is not given. But we can deduce from test. For first case:\nnodes: root (depth0, non-removable), n1 (depth1, non-removable), n2 (depth1, removable), n3 (depth1, non-removable), n4 (depth2, removable, parent n2), n5 (depth2, non-removable), n6 (depth2, removable, parent n1). Required symbols: root, s1.\n\nWhich nodes are candidates? n2, n4, n6 are removable. Depth order: deepest first: depths 2: n4, n6 have same depth; tie break by ID: 'n4' < 'n6'? Lexicographically 'n4' < 'n6'. So order: n4, n6, then n2 (depth1). But need to consider that after removing a node, its children (if any) are also removed. Also, cannot remove a node if it would make required symbol missing. And cannot remove a node that has retained children? Actually \"never remove a node with retained children\" – this means if after some removals, a node still has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove that node. That's important: we must check children that are still in the tree (not removed yet).\n\nLet's simulate first case manually to see plausible output.\n\nInitial tree: root (depth0, symbol root), n1 (depth1, s1), n2 (depth1, s2), n3 (depth1, s0), n4 (depth2, s1, parent n2), n5 (depth2, s2, parent n3), n6 (depth2, s0, parent n1). Required: root, s1.\n\nWe consider nodes in order: depth2: n4 and n6. n4: removable true, not root, symbol s1 is required, but required symbol s1 must remain represented – if we remove n4, there is still n1 with symbol s1, so that's ok. Does n4 have retained children? It has none (depth2 leaf). So can remove n4. Remove it. Then node n6: removable, symbol s0 (not required), no children, can remove? Check required symbols: still root and s1 (n1 still there). So remove n6. Then depth1: n2 (depth1) is removable, symbol s2 not required. Does it have retained children? Its child n4 was removed, so no retained children. Can we remove n2? Required symbols still present (root, n1 has s1). So remove n2. Then depth1: n1 and n3 are not removable. No more candidates. Final retained: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted retained IDs: ['n1','n3','n5','root']? Actually root is also retained. Sort: 'n1','n3','n5','root'. Removed order: ['n4','n6','n2'].\n\nBut wait, there's also n5 at depth2 (non-removable) and n3 (non-removable). So retained set is root, n1, n3, n5. That seems plausible. But is there any rule that we should also consider removing parent after children? That is what we did. The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That matches.\n\nNow we need to implement exactly. The algorithm:\n\n- Build adjacency: parents, children (list of child IDs).\n- Keep a set of removed_ids initially empty.\n- Build a list of node IDs sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending (lexicographic). We'll use that as processing order.\n- For each node in that order:\n    - Skip if already removed, or if removable is False (or if id == 'root'? Actually root may not be flagged removable, but check just in case.)\n    - Check: Is this node required? (by checking if its symbol is in required_symbols). Actually requirement is on symbol, not node. So we cannot remove a node if its symbol is required? Wait: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It says \"a required symbol\" – this could mean a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. So we cannot remove any node that has a required symbol. In our example, n1 has symbol s1 which is required, so n1 cannot be removed (even if it were removable, which it isn't). So we must check: if node['symbol'] in required_symbols, then skip removal.\n    - Also check: does the node have any children that are still retained (not in removed_ids)? If yes, skip.\n    - Now tentatively remove: add node to removed_ids, then check if all required symbols are still represented (i.e., for each required symbol, there exists at least one node with that symbol that is not removed). If yes, keep removal (add to removed list in order, continue). Else, revert removal (remove from removed_ids).\n\nAfter processing all nodes, the remaining nodes (not in removed_ids) are retained. Sorted list of their IDs.\n\nReturn {\"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained}.\n\nEdge cases: nodes with no parent? root has null parent. Also, we must be careful about the order of processing: we iterate once over the sorted list, but removal of a parent later might be affected by prior removal of its children. However the order we process ensures deeper nodes first, so by the time we consider a parent, its children have already been considered for removal. That's correct because we need to check \"retained children\" – children not removed. Since we process children first, they may have been removed already. So when we consider parent, its children set (original children) minus removed_ids will give retained children. So condition works.\n\nWe must not mutate the case dict; we'll work with copies.\n\nNow implement the solve function.\n\nReturn the implementation JSON.\n\nNote: The output must be a JSON object with exactly 'implementation' key, and its value a string of Python code. No additional text.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2057,"completion_tokens":2225,"total_tokens":4282,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1761},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":9},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}